QUESTION ONE – Java OOP-Based Transport Management System (15 Marks)



Objective:



Design and implement a Java prototype for a University Transport Management System (UTMS) showcasing OOP principles.



a)	Implementation Guidelines by OOP Principle:



(i)	Abstraction – Abstract Classes/Interfaces



Use abstract classes or interfaces to define templates:





Public abstract class TransportService {

    Abstract void schedule();

    Abstract void assignDriver();

}



Or



Public interface TransportOperations {

    Void schedule();

    Void assignDriver();

}



Role: Defines a general contract for transport services, hiding the logic of each specific vehicle or schedule type.



(ii)	Encapsulation – Use of Private Fields with Getters/Setters



Public class Driver {

    Private String licenseNumber;

    Private String name;



    Public String getLicenseNumber() {

        Return licenseNumber;

    }



    Public void setLicenseNumber(String licenseNumber) {

        This.licenseNumber = licenseNumber;

    }

}



Role: Protects sensitive information like credentials and vehicle/driver info.



(iii)	Inheritance – Base User Class and Subclasses



Public class User {

    Protected String name;

    Protected String id;



    Public void requestTransport() {

        System.out.println(“Requesting transport...”);

    }

}



Public class Student extends User {

    // Extra attributes/methods specific to students

}



Public class Lecturer extends User {

    // Extra attributes/methods specific to lecturers

}



Public class TransportOfficer extends User {

    // Admin functionalities

}



Role: Promotes code reuse and logical structure.



(iv)	Polymorphism – Method Overriding



@Override

Public void requestTransport() {

    System.out.println(“Student requesting transport for a class.”);

}



Role: Enables different user types to request transport differently at runtime.



(v)	Interfaces for Behavior



Public interface Schedulable {

    Void schedule();

}



Public class Bus implements Schedulable {

    Public void schedule() {

        System.out.println(“Bus scheduled.”);

    }

}



Role: Represents common behaviors that can be shared across multiple classes.



(vi)	Override requestTransport() in Subclasses



Public class Lecturer extends User {

    @Override

    Public void requestTransport() {

        System.out.println(“Lecturer requesting transport for a seminar.”);

    }

}



Role: Demonstrates different request behavior.



(vii)	Method Overloading – assignDriver()



Public class TransportManager {

    Public void assignDriver(String vehicleType) {

        System.out.println(“Assigning driver for “ + vehicleType);

    }



    Public void assignDriver(String vehicleType, String shiftTime) {

        System.out.println(“Assigning driver for “ + vehicleType + “ during “ + shiftTime);

    }

}



Role: Different forms of assigning drivers depending on input parameters.



